olor:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Coarse temporal precision; cannot resolve reliable word-level boundaries.</span></p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Speaker Diarization and Attribution</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Speaker diarization identifies "who spoke when," converting flat transcripts into structured, multi-speaker dialogue. This attribution is critical for interviews, podcasts, panel discussions, and dramatic narratives.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The open-source reference for speaker diarization is pyannote.audio 3.1, developed by HervÃ© Bredin et al. The architecture operates across three sequential stages:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_2-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">A neural segmentation model (pyannote/segmentation-3.0) slides a 10-second receptive window across 16 kHz mono audio in small increments. The model applies powerset classification, assigning each frame to one of several mutually exclusive speaker combinations. This allows the system to resolve overlapping speech between up to two speakers simultaneouslyâ€”a major challenge in earlier diarization systems.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Fixed-dimensional speaker embeddings are extracted across identified speech turns using an ECAPA-TDNN architecture.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering groups matching embeddings across the timeline, assigning unified speaker identifiers (such as SPEAKER_00, SPEAKER_01) throughout the recording.</span></li></ul><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">On standard benchmarks, pyannote.audio 3.1 achieves a Diarization Error Rate (DER) of 11% to 14% on the AMI meeting corpus and 9% to 11% on VoxConverse YouTube audio. Diarization throughput reaches approximately 50 times real-time on an NVIDIA A100 GPU, consuming roughly 0.8 GB of VRAM. However, deployment requires managing specific licensing terms: while the Pyannote codebase is released under the MIT license, the pretrained model weights on Hugging Face (pyannote/speaker-diarization-3.1 and pyannote/segmentation-3.0) are gated, requiring user agreement acceptance and runtime API token authorization.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Diarization tracks must be reconciled with transcribed text through temporal intersection. Transcribed words have discrete start and end timestamps, while the diarization pipeline outputs speaker turn intervals. The orchestration engine calculates the temporal overlap between each word's duration [w_{\text{start}}, w_{\text{end}}] and the diarization segments [s_{\text{start}}, s_{\text{end}}], assigning the speaker ID with the greatest overlap. Words falling within overlapping speech intervals are flagged with primary and secondary speaker labels to alert downstream layout engines to format concurrent dialogue.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Caption Generation and Ergonomics</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Raw transcripts segmented solely by Voice Activity Detection or arbitrary pauses are unsuitable for viewing. Subtitle generation requires balancing visual layout, reading ergonomics, and audio synchronization to prevent cognitive overload.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Subtitles must adhere to strict spatial and temporal limits informed by professional standards, such as BBC Subtitle Guidelines and Netflix Timed Text Style Guides. On horizontal 16:9 displays, subtitles should not exceed 37 characters per line (CPL) across a maximum of two lines. For vertical 9:16 displays (such as YouTube Shorts), line lengths should be constrained to 20 to 25 characters on a single line to prevent overlap with native mobile platform UI overlays.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Display pacing must align with human reading comprehension limits. For standard video, reading speeds should remain between 12 and 17 characters per second (CPS). Kinetic captions on vertical video may reach 20 CPS, but higher rates impair readability. Display durations require corresponding boundaries: events shorter than 1.0 second can flash past too quickly for the viewer's visual processing, while cues exceeding 5 to 6 seconds can feel sluggish and fall out of sync with speech pacing. Gaps between adjacent subtitle events should either be eliminated completely (cut-to-cut) or maintain a minimum gap of 2 to 4 frames (66 to 133 milliseconds at 30 fps) to prevent visual flickering that forces the viewer's eyes to re-scan the cue.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Line breaks must align with grammatical and syntactic structures rather than arbitrary character thresholds. Breaking lines between closely coupled syntactic units disrupts reading flow:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_3-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Line breaks should never separate compound nouns (e.g., splitting "Grand" and "Canyon"), split an auxiliary verb from its primary verb (e.g., separating "has" and "completed"), or detach a preposition from its associated noun phrase (e.g., splitting "across" and "the river").</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Line breaks are best placed after terminal punctuation, at coordinate conjunctions, or immediately preceding relative pronouns and major clause boundaries.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">In two-line horizontal captions, a "pyramid" layoutâ€”where the upper line is shorter than the lower lineâ€”provides a stable visual lower third that minimizes screen obstruction.</span></li></ul><table style="border-spacing:0;border-collapse:collapse;margin-right:auto"><thead><tr style="height:0pt"><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:117pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Subtitle Parameter</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:117pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Horizontal Video (16:9)</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:117pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;