0000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:93.6pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Yes (Time-stamped spans)</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:93.6pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">High (XML layout specs)</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:93.6pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Moderate (SMPTE extensions)</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:93.6pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Broadcast and enterprise streaming distribution.</span></p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Multilingual Captions and Timing Transferability</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Producing multilingual captions highlights a fundamental linguistic constraint: word-level timing data cannot be mapped directly across languages through text translation alone.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Grammatical syntax and constituent word orders vary significantly between language families. English, for example, follows Subject-Verb-Object (SVO) order ("The researcher observed the comet"), whereas Hindi and many Indo-Aryan languages follow Subject-Object-Verb (SOV) order ("à¤¶à¥‹à¤§à¤•à¤°à¥à¤¤à¤¾ à¤¨à¥‡ à¤§à¥‚à¤®à¤•à¥‡à¤¤à¥ à¤•à¤¾ à¤…à¤µà¤²à¥‹à¤•à¤¨ à¤•à¤¿à¤¯à¤¾"). Because verbs, nouns, and modifiers occupy different positions in the sentence, transferring English word timestamps onto translated Hindi words results in complete visual-auditory desynchronization.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Furthermore, translation changes character and syllable counts. Translating English into Spanish, French, or Hindi often causes text expansions of 15% to 30%, whereas translating into Japanese or Chinese contracts spatial requirements. In non-segmenting scripts like Chinese, Japanese, and Thai, line breaks must follow script-specific rules (such as respecting Japanese </span><span style="font-style:italic">Kinsoku Shori</span><span>&nbsp;punctuation rules) rather than whitespace boundaries. Additionally, Devanagari script features conjunct consonants, </span><span style="font-style:italic">matras</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;(dependent vowel signs), and vertical diacritics that require increased line-height spacing in rendering engines to prevent vertical clipping.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">To preserve timing accuracy across multilingual captions, the orchestration pipeline must adhere to specific handling rules:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_5-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Translations must be anchored to parent sentence or semantic clause boundaries, preserving top-level start and end times while recalculating intra-sentence text wrapping.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">If word-level karaoke animation is required for translated captions, word timestamps must be proportionally interpolated across characters within the clause boundary as an approximation.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">When localization involves synthetic voice dubbing via Text-to-Speech (TTS), the localized audio must pass through a dedicated forced-alignment model (such as IndicMFA or multilingual Wav2Vec2) to establish authentic word timestamps in the target language.</span></li></ul><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Script and Narration Quality Assurance</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">In automated content generation, synthetic voice narration from TTS systems or recorded human voice-over must be verified against the approved production script. Discrepancies such as dropped sentences, mispronounced acronyms, phonetic substitutions, or hallucinated additions must be caught before final video rendering.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Speech verification relies on string edit-distance metrics calculated over normalized token sequences. The standard evaluation metric is Word Error Rate (WER), defined mathematically as:</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">\text{WER} = \frac{S + D + I}{N} = \frac{S + D + I}{S + D + C}</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">where S represents substitutions, D represents deletions (words present in the script but missing from the audio), I represents insertions (hallucinated or extraneous words emitted in audio), and C represents correct matches relative to total reference script words N. Character Error Rate (CER) applies this Levenshtein distance formulation at the character level:</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">\text{CER} = \frac{S_c + D_c + I_c}{N_c}</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">CER is particularly useful for evaluating morphologically rich languages, compound words, and Indic scripts where minor phonological substitutions might not register clearly at the whole-word level.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Automated verification pipelines leverage the JiWER library (version 4.0+). Built with a C++ RapidFuzz backend, JiWER efficiently aligns long-form text sequences. A key feature in JiWER 4.0 is its handling of empty reference strings without division-by-zero errors. This allows verification gates to check silent audio segments: any words detected during unvoiced intervals register as insertions (I &gt; 0), reliably flagging hallucinated speech.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Before computing edit distance, both the approved script and the STT transcript must undergo bidirectional text normalization. Inverse Text Normalization (ITN) converts numeric expressions, dates, currency symbols, and abbreviations into spoken word equivalents (for example, mapping "$45" to "forty-five dollars" and "Dr." to "Doctor"). Without this step, standard numeric formatting differences trigger false-positive QA rejections.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Automated Narration QA Gates apply deterministic thresholds to incoming audio:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_6-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">A Critical Verification Gate rejects audio if the overall WER exceeds 3.0% or if any sequence of three or more consecutive words is omitted.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">A Proper Noun Verification Gate cross-references named entities from the production script against the transcript to ensure accurate pronunciation and transcription.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">A Delivery Rate Gate evaluates the spoken pace (\text{Words} / \text{Duration}), flagging narration that falls below 120 WPM (unnaturally slow) or exceeds 210 WPM (uncomfortably fast).</span></li></ul><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Canonical Temporal Representation</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">A production microservice architecture requires a standardized, lossless data interchange format. While several formats exist across the media industry, their technical trade-offs vary considerably:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_7-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">OpenTimelineIO (OTIO) is the open-source standard for editorial timeline exchange across digital content creation applications. However, OTIO lacks an official subtitle schema; pull request #805 and issue #62 have remained open and unresolved for years. In current production pipelines, developers work around this limitation by converting subtitle cues into timeline Marker instances attached to video tracks, storing cue text within metadata dictionaries. While this enables timeline conforming, it lacks native support for hierarchical word-level timing or typography properties.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Praat TextGrid and ELAN Annotation Files (.eaf) support multi-tiered hierarchical alignments (phones, syllables, words, clauses), but their XML and custom ASCII formats are verbose, parsing-intensive, and poorly suited for modern web microservices.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">A typed, hierarchical JSON representation provides the cleanest solution for microservice architectures, offering native validation, lightweight serialization, and direct compatibility with downstream ASS and WebVTT compilers.</span></li></ul><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The internal canonical schema records temporal boundaries as integer milliseconds (start_ms, end_ms), eliminating floating-point rounding errors across varied timeline frame rates (such as 23.976, 25, 29.97, and 60 fps):</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Courier&quot;;font-style:normal">{<br> &nbsp;"$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",<br> &nbsp;"version": "1.0.0",<br> &nbsp;"metadata": {<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;"media_id": "seq_v04_narration",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;"duration_ms": 14250,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;"sample_rate": 16000,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;"language": "en-US",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;"pipeline_run_id": "run_9841ab"<br> &nbsp;},<br> &nbsp;"speakers": [<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;{<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"id": "SPK_00",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"label": "Host",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"confidence": 0.98<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;}<br> &nbsp;],<br> &nbsp;"segments": [<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;{<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"seg[span_30](start_span)[span_30](end_span)[span_35](start_span)[span_35](end_span)ment_id": "seg_0001",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"speaker": "SPK_00",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"start_ms": 120,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"end_ms": 3450,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"text": "The solar flare erupted with unprecedented power.",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"confidence": 0.96,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"words": [<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;{<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"word": "The",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"start_ms": 120,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"end_ms": 280,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"confidence": 0.99,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"phonemes": [<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;{"phone": "DH", "start_ms": 120, "end_ms": 200},<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;{"phone": "AH", "start_ms": 200, "end_ms": 280}<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;},<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;{<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"word": "solar",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"start_ms": 310,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"end_ms": 680,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"confidence": 0.98,<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"phonemes": [<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;{"phone": "S", "start_ms": 310, "end_ms": 420},<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;{"phone": "OW", "start_ms": 420, "end_ms": 550},<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;{"phone": "L", "start_ms": 550, "end_ms": 610},<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;{"phone": "ER", "start_ms": 610, "end_ms": 680}<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;}<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;}<br> &nbsp;]<br>}<br></span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Scene and Script Synchronization</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The canonical temporal transcript connects written production scripts to non-linear video editing timelines. Orchestrating these media assets requires a clear division between deterministic temporal computation and LLM-driven creative reasoning.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Deterministic calculations should handle low-level timeline operations without introducing LLM non-determinism:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_8-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Scene Boundary Resolution: When a script links Scene 03 to Paragraph 03, the pipeline queries the temporal transcript for the first and last word tokens in that paragraph, establishing exact audio start and end timestamps.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Frame Snapping: Millisecond timestamps are mapped to discrete timeline frame boundaries using the project's target frame rate: \text{Frame Number} = \text{round}\left(\frac{\text{Time}_{\text{ms}}}{1000} \times \text{FPS}\right) This ensures visual cuts, B-roll transitions, and graphics align with full frame boundaries rather than occurring mid-frame.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Kinetic Typography Triggers: Words marked for visual emphasis (e.g., bold or italicized keywords in the script) have their exact start timestamps queried from the transcript, triggering motion graphics or text callouts on the correct video frame.</span></li></ul><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The Hermes orchestration engine focuses on higher-level directorial reasoning:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_9-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Narrative Pacing and Cut Placement: Selecting appropriate moments during speech pauses to insert B-roll clips without interrupting dialogue flow.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Multi-Camera Angle Switching: Using speaker transition events from the diarization track to guide camera cuts between dialogue participants.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Scene Timing Adjustments: Pacing visual assets and motion sequences to match variations in voice-over speed across takes.</span></li></ul><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Local and Open-Source Systems Evaluation</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">For self-hosted production environments, selecting speech-to-text, forced alignment, and diarization systems requires balancing inference throughput, VRAM consumption, and licensing requirements.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Faster-Whisper (using the Large-v3 Turbo model) is well-suited for self-hosted transcription. Operating on CTranslate2, it processes audio 15 to 30 times faster than real-time on modern GPUs while consuming approximately 1.6 GB of VRAM. It includes native Silero VAD integration, which prevents hallucination loops during long pauses. Faster-Whisper is released under the permissive MIT license, permitting unencumbered commercial use.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">WhisperX builds upon Faster-Whisper by adding VAD-based batch processing and a Wav2Vec2 CTC forced-alignment pass. This pipeline achieves throughput between 20 and 50 times real-time on an RTX 4090 or A100 GPU with 4 to 6 GB of VRAM allocation. The core WhisperX framework is licensed under BSD-2-Clause, but its dependencies include components with varying licenses that must be managed during deployment.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">FunASR SenseVoiceSmall is a strong candidate for low-latency transcription in supported languages (Mandarin, Cantonese, English, Japanese, Korean). With 234 million parameters, it runs at up to 50 times real-time on modest GPU hardware, consuming only ~1.2 GB of VRAM. The codebase is released under the MIT license, while model weights follow the FunASR Model License Agreement, which allows commercial use upon application.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The Montreal Forced Aligner (MFA 3.x) provides high-accuracy phonetic alignment using Kaldi HMM-GMM architectures. Because it is CPU-bound, MFA runs near real-time across 8 to 16 CPU cores, consuming roughly 4 GB of RAM. While MFA is free and open-source under the GitHub MontrealCorpusTools organization, its heavy filesystem dependencies and complex Kaldi toolchain make containerized deployment and horizontal scaling challenging in cloud environments.</span></p><table style="border-spacing:0;border-collapse:collapse;margin-right:auto"><thead><tr style="height:0pt"><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:78pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-sty