ght-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:78pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Torchaudio ForcedAligner</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:78pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Native PyTorch C++/CUDA CTC core</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:78pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&gt;100x real-time (GPU)</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:78pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">~500 MB VRAM / 1 GB RAM</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:78pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">BSD-2-Clause (PyTorch core)</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:78pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Very High. Integral component of the PyTorch framework.</span></p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hosted APIs Evaluation</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">When production demands exceed local hardware capacity, or when a managed infrastructure layer is preferred, hosted speech APIs offer scalable transcription, alignment, and diarization services.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">AssemblyAI provides a comprehensive voice API suite. Its pre-recorded Universal-2 model is priced at $0.15 per audio hour ($0.0025/min), while the flagship Universal-3.5 Pro model costs $0.21 per audio hour ($0.0035/min). Speaker diarization is available as an unbundled add-on for $0.02 per hour. The API supports per-second billing with no minimum duration thresholds, reducing costs on short audio segments. AssemblyAI returns precise word- and character-level timestamps alongside confidence scores, and supports asynchronous workflows via webhook callbacks. By default, accounts participate in a model improvement training program; organizations requiring strict privacy must request a formal opt-out or sign an enterprise agreement.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Deepgram specializes in low-latency speech recognition. Pricing for its flagship Nova-3 model starts at $0.46 per hour ($0.0077/min) on pay-as-you-go tiers, with speaker diarization adding $0.12 per hour ($0.002/min). Its previous-generation Nova-2 model is priced at $0.35 per hour ($0.0058/min). Deepgram returns word-level timestamps and confidence metrics, supporting high-throughput batch operations and streaming WebSockets. Commercial accounts can enable zero-data-retention modes, ensuring customer audio is discarded immediately following processing.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Google Cloud Speech-to-Text V2 features the Chirp and Chirp 2 foundation models. Chirp batch transcription costs $0.24 per hour ($0.004/min) through Dynamic Batch processing, while standard real-time recognition costs $0.96 per hour ($0.016/min). Diarization adds an unbundled fee of $0.36 per hour. The service integrates with Google Cloud Storage and Pub/Sub notifications, operating under enterprise GCP terms that prevent customer data from being used to train foundation models.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The OpenAI Audio API provides transcription using whisper-1 at $0.36 per hour ($0.006/min), along with newer models including gpt-4o-mini-transcribe at $0.18 per hour ($0.003/min) and gpt-4o-transcribe at $0.60 per hour ($0.010/min). The base whisper-1 endpoint does not support speaker diarization, requiring multi-speaker workloads to use diarization-capable variants (e.g., gpt-4o-transcribe-diarize). Timestamp accuracy on the Whisper API is coarse, as results reflect the model's native segment-level timestamp tokens. Furthermore, the API imposes a 25 MB file size limit and lacks native webhook callbacks, requiring client-side polling architectures.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>| Hosted Provider | Flagship Model | Async Batch Rate (Base STT) | Diarization Add-on Rate | Word Timestamp Accuracy | Webhook / Async Architecture | Data Privacy &amp; Training Terms | | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | | </span><span style="font-weight:700">AssemblyAI</span><span>&nbsp;| Universal-2 / 3.5 Pro | $0.15 / hr (Univ-2) $0.21 / hr (3.5 Pro) | +$0.02 / hr | Precise character and word start/end times | Robust webhooks, REST polling, per-second billing | Opt-out required to prevent data use in model training; enterprise zero-retention available. | | </span><span style="font-weight:700">Deepgram</span><span>&nbsp;| Nova-3 / Nova-2 | $0.46 / hr (Nova-3) $0.35 / hr (Nova-2) | +$0.12 / hr ($0.002/min) | Direct CTC word-level timestamps | Asynchronous callbacks, WebSockets, REST endpoints | Zero data retention available on commercial tiers; SOC 2 certified. | | </span><span style="font-weight:700">Google Cloud STT V2</span><span>&nbsp;| Chirp / Chirp 2 | $0.24 / hr (Dynamic Batch) $0.96 / hr (Standard) | +$0.36 / hr | Reliable word timestamps across 100+ languages | Cloud Storage triggers, Pub/Sub event queues | Enterprise cloud terms; customer audio is not used for model training. | | </span><span style="font-weight:700">OpenAI Audio</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;| Whisper-1 / gpt-4o-transcribe | $0.18 / hr (4o-mini) $0.36 / hr (Whisper-1) | Not available on Whisper-1; available on 4o-diarize | Approximate segment and word boundaries | HTTP POST polling only; 25 MB request body limit | Data submitted through the API is not used for model training. |</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">MCP, Skill, and Agent Integrations</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Integrating speech alignment into agent-driven orchestration environments (such as Hermes) requires clear operational interfaces and well-defined boundaries.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Several Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers and command-line tools support speech processing tasks:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_a-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The arcaputo3/mcp-server-wh[span_180](start_span)[span_180](end_span)[span_184](start_span)[span_184](end_span)i[span_188](start_span)[span_188](end_span)sper repository exposes OpenAI-compatible transcription endpoints over the standard MCP protocol. It provides basic transcription, but lacks native hooks for forced alignment, multi-speaker diarization, or styled subtitle formatting.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The sandraschi/speech-mcp project provides broader capabilities, integrating multiple backends including FunASR SenseVoiceSmall, Whisper variants, and Voice Activity Detection. It allows agents to request speech transcription and acoustic event tags (such as music or applause) through a unified interface.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The capcut-cli automation skill provides programmatic editing tools for short-form video. It includes timeline linting rules that evaluate reading speeds (flagging cues exceeding 20 characters per second), verify safe-area margins on 9:16 vertical video (flagging text placed within the outer 15% where platform UI elements appear), adjust CJK line wrapping, and translate subtitle tracks into OpenTimelineIO marker tracks.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The premiere-pro-mcp suite exposes non-linear editing commands to autonomous agents. Because Adobe Premiere Pro does not support programmatic text clip creation directly through scripting APIs, the MCP server automates importing pre-compiled .srt or .vtt sidecars into dedicated caption tracks.</span></li></ul><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">When designing MCP interfaces for audio processing, binary media files should not be passed directly across JSON-RPC protocol connections. Transferring large base64-encoded audio clips causes high serialization overhead, inflates agent context windows, and increases latency. Instead, agents should pass filesystem paths or object-storage URIs (s3://..., file:///media/...), receiving compact, structured temporal JSON transcripts that downstream orchestration components can reference efficiently.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Existing Project Resource Reassessment</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Reviewing the production engine's existing captioning and audio processing assets helps identify w