XP-01: Boundary Precision of CTC vs. Cross-Attention DTW</span></h3><ul class="lst-kix_list_c-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hypothesis: CTC-based forced alignment achieves higher timestamp accuracy than cross-attention DTW on rapid narration (&gt;190 WPM), maintaining temporal error within \le [span_150](start_span)[span_150](end_span)30 milliseconds of ground truth.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Test Procedure: Run 50 rapid-tempo narration audio files through WhisperX (Wav2Vec2 CTC) and whisper-timestamped (cross-attention DTW). Compare generated word start and end timestamps against manual phonetically aligned ground truth.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Evidence Required: Millisecond offset distribution matrices for word onset and offset boundaries across both engines.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Success Criteria: At least 95% of CTC-aligned word starts fall within \le 30 milliseconds of ground truth, compared to \le 80 milliseconds for cross-attention DTW.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Prior Insufficiency: Published literature demonstrates general improvements on standard corpora, but lacks comparative benchmarks on high-tempo, voice-over speech.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Experiment VAL-EXP-02: Overlapping Speech Resolution in Pyannote 3.1</span></h3><ul class="lst-kix_list_d-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hypothesis: Pyannote 3.1's powerset segmentation model maintains a Diarization Error Rate (DER) below 12% on multi-speaker studio cross-talk without manual speaker count hints.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Test Procedure: Process 20 multi-speaker podcast audio tracks featuring unconstrained conversational overlap through Pyannote 3.1. Benchmark speaker turn intervals against hand-annotated reference RTTM files.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Evidence Required: Detailed DER metrics isolating Missed Speech, False Alarm, and Speaker Confusion error rates. * Success Criteria: Overall DER remains \le 12.0\%, with overlapping speech detection accuracy exceeding 80%.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Prior Insufficiency: Published DER figures focus on standard academic benchmarks (AMI, VoxConverse), which differ acoustically from studio podcast cross-talk.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Experiment VAL-EXP-03: Hinglish Code-Switching Transcription Accuracy</span></h3><ul class="lst-kix_list_e-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hypothesis: AI4Bharat's IndicWhisper achieves at least a 25% relative reduction in Word Error Rate over Whisper Large-v3 on conversational Hindi-English speech.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Test Procedure: Transcribe 100 conversational Hinglish video excerpts using both IndicWhisper and OpenAI Whisper Large-v3. Calculate WER and CER against verbatim ground truth transcripts.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Evidence Required: Comparative WER and CER distributions across pure Hindi phrases, English loan words, and mixed syntactic clauses.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Success Criteria: IndicWhisper demonstrates a \ge 2[span_121](start_span)[span_121](end_span)[span_128](start_span)[span_128](end_span)5\% relative WER reduction over Whisper Large-v3 on code-mixed segments.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Prior Insufficiency: IndicWhisper benchmarks primarily evaluate read-speech datasets (Kathbath), requiring validation on conversational YouTube audio.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Experiment VAL-EXP-04: Automated Script QA Gate Threshold Calibration</span></h3><ul class="lst-kix_list_f-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hypothesis: An automated JiWER QA gate with bidirectional Inverse Text Normalization can detect 100% of omitted clauses and synthetic speech hallucinations while maintaining a false-rejection rate below 1%.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Test Procedure: Generate 100 synthetic narration tracks with intentionally introduced errors (clause omissions, phonetic mispronunciations, number formatting variations, and silence-induced hallucinations). Process the audio through Faster-Whisper and the JiWER QA pipeline.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Evidence Required: Confusion matrix classifying true positive detections, false positive rejections, and false negative misses across error categories.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Success Criteria: 100% detection of dropped clauses (\ge 3 consecutive words) and zero false rejections on valid numeric formats.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Prior Insufficiency: Existing JiWER documentation covers string comparison mechanics, but lacks calibrated operational thresholds for automated narration QA gates.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Experiment VAL-EXP-05: FFmpeg libass Kinetic Rendering Under Load</span></h3><ul class="lst-kix_list_10-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hypothesis: FFmpeg's libass filter can render complex, word-level kinetic subtitle animations at 4K and 1080p resolutions without frame drops or memory leaks.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Test Procedure: Compile 50 Advanced SubStation Alpha (.ass) scripts containing dense {\k...} karaoke tags, coordinate movement vectors, and alpha fading effects. Render the files across high-resolution video streams via FFmpeg's libass filter.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Evidence Required: Render throughput logs, GPU/CPU utilization profiles, memory stability metrics, and visual frame comparisons.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Success Criteria: Stable render speeds without frame drops, memory consumption remaining below 2 GB RSS, and accurate visual text alignment across all test sequences.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Prior Insufficiency: Complex ASS animation tags can occasionally stall rendering threads in older libass builds, requiring validation on modern containerized environments.</span></li></ul><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Genuine Architectural Gaps</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Evaluating modern speech and caption tooling reveals three notable architectural gaps:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_11-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The lack of a standardized subtitle schema within OpenTimelineIO. PR #805 and Issue #62 remain unmerged, forcing pipelines to rely on marker tracks and custom metadata dictionaries to pass subtitle data into non-linear editing systems.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Automated cross-lingual caption timing transfer remains an unsolved problem. Syntactic word order divergence between language families (such as SVO in English versus SOV in Indic languages) prevents 1:1 word-level timestamp mapping. Reliable word-level timing for translated subtitles currently requires generating localized TTS audio and running an independent forced-alignment pass.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Pretrained CTC forced-alignment acoustic models for Hindi-English code-mixed speech are scarce. While English Wav2Vec2 models are mature, production-ready CTC models trained specifically on conversational Hinglish remain limited, often requiring fallback to HMM-GMM aligners like IndicMFA or character-level multilingual models.</span></li></ul><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Recommended Temporal and Caption Architecture</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">To provide Hermes with reliable timing data, the production engine decouples transcription, temporal alignment, quality verification, and subtitle rendering into discrete stages:</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Courier&quot;;font-style:normal">Audio / Video Stream<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v<br>[Voice Activity Detection] (Silero VAD)<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v<br>[Speech Recognition] (Faster-Whisper Engine)<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v<br>[Narration Verification Gate] (JiWER Levenshtein Engine)<br> &nbsp;- Compares transcript against approved production script<br> &nbsp;- Rejects audio exceeding error thresholds (WER &gt; 3%)<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v<br>[Forced Alignment Engine] (Torchaudio / Wav2Vec2 CTC)<br> &nbsp;- Computes millisecond word and character boundaries<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; +---------------------------------------+<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; | &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v<br>[Speaker Diarization] &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; [Temporal Transcript Compiler]<br> (Pyannote.audio 3.1) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;- Assembles Canonical JSON<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; | &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - Integer millisecond timestamps<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; +---------------------------------------+<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; [Canonical Temporal Transcript]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; +-----------------------+-----------------------+<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; | &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v<br>[Caption Layout Policy Engine] &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;[span_264](start_span)[span_264](end_span)[span_268](start_span)[span_268](end_span) &nbsp; &nbsp; [Timeline Synchronizer]<br> &nbsp;- CPL and CPS reading limits &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - Snaps boundaries to video frames<br> &nbsp;- Safe-area margins (16:9 vs 9:16) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - Maps Scene / Shot IDs<br> &nbsp;- Syntactic line-breaking logic &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;- Emits OTIO Track Markers<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; | &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;[span_265](start_span)[span_265](end_span)[span_269](start_span)[span_269](end_span) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v<br>[Format Compiler] &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;[Hermes Editorial Engine]<br> &nbsp;- SRT / WebVTT sidecar exports &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;- Video cut-point alignment<br> &nbsp;- Styled ASS subtitle generation &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;- Visual asset conforming<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; v<br>[FFmpeg libass Renderer]<br> &nbsp;- Burnt-in video composition<br></span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The pipeline operates through sequential, deterministic steps:</span></p><ol class="lst-kix_list_12-0 start" start="1" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Audio Ingestion and Pre-Filtering: Spoken audio is downmixed to 16 kHz mono WAV format. Silero VAD detects speech segments, stripping unvoiced intervals to protect downstream models from silence-induced hallucination loops.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Dual-Path Transcription and Verification: For scripted content, Faster-Whisper generates an initial draft transcript, and the JiWER QA Gate compares it against the approved production script using normalized Levenshtein distance metrics. If the Word Error Rate is \le 3.0\% with no missing clauses, the audio is verified for alignment. Audio that fails verification is rejected for re-recording. Unscripted audio bypasses the script gate and is transcribed directly via Faster-Whisper or IndicWhisper.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Frame-Accurate CTC Forced Alignment: The verified text sequence and audio stream pass into a GPU-accelerated CTC alignment module (using Torchaudio or WhisperX). The module computes millisecond-level start and end timestamps for every word and character in the recording.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Speaker Attribution: Multi-speaker recordings run concurrently through pyannote.audio 3.1. The resulting speaker turn intervals are cross-referenced with the word timestamps, assigning speaker IDs to each word token.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Canonical Temporal Assembly: The alignment and speaker data are compiled into a unified, canonical temporal JSON file, establishing the timing reference for all downstream production workers.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Caption Layout and Segmentation: The layout engine parses the canonical JSON to generate readable subtitle cues based on the target display format: horizontal 16:9 cues are capped at 37 CPL across two lines, while vertical 9:16 cues are capped at 20 to 25 CPL on a single line within safe-area boundaries. Line breaks are placed strictly at natural syntactic phrase boundaries.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Format Compilation and Rendering: The layout engine compiles captions into target delivery formats: clean WebVTT or SRT files for platform sidecars, or Advanced SubStation Alpha (.ass) files containing pixel coordinates and {\k...} karaoke tags for kinetic text highlights. Styled ASS subtitles are composited onto the video stream using FFmpeg's libass filter.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Editorial Conforming: The canonical temporal JSON is converted into OpenTimelineIO marker tracks. These markers allow the Hermes director framework to conform editorial cut points, pace B-roll transitions around natural speech pauses, and synchronize visual emphasis triggers to the spoken audio.</span></li></ol><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Primary Source Verification and Evidence Standards</span></h2><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">To maintain high architectural reliability, technical claims in this evaluation were verified against official primary sources. Speech recognition architectures and drift patterns were confirmed through OpenAI model cards and Oxford University's WhisperX technical publications. Dynamic Time Warping mechanics and licensing constraints were verified directly in the linto-ai/whisper-timestamped repository and GNU license documentation. Acoustic model and forced-alignment metrics were corroborated using Montreal Forced Aligner documentation and published phonetic benchmark studies on the PBCM corpus. Diarization mechanisms, Diarization Error Rates, and powerset classification frameworks were verified through publications by HervÃ© Bredin and Pyannote documentation. Pricing, rate limits, and data retention policies were drawn directly from official AssemblyAI, Deepgram, and Google Cloud documentation. Finally, subtitle standards, reading speeds, and OpenTimelineIO schema limitations were confirmed through BBC guidelines, Netflix specifications, and the official Academy Software Foundation OpenTimelineIO repository. Capabilities lacking sufficient runtime documentation have been designated as NOT VERIFIED to avoid speculation.</span></p><h4 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:12pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Works cited</span></h4><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">1. GitHub - linto-ai/whisper-timestamped: Multilingual Automatic, https://github.com/linto-ai/whisper-timestamped 2. WhisperX: Time-Accurate Speech Transcription of Long-Form Audio, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:fece4192-95b7-4db8-a018-3cf728040194/files/swm117q47j 3. (PDF) CrisperWhisper: Accurate Timestamps on Verbatim Speech, https://www.researchgate.net/publication/383530105_CrisperWhisper_Accurate_Timestamps_on_Verbatim_Speech_Transcriptions 4. capcut-cli - Yarn Classic, https://classic.yarnpkg.com/en/package/capcut-cli 5. Subtitles &amp; captions Â· Issue #62 - GitHub, https://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/OpenTimelineIO/issues/805/linked_closing_reference?reference_location=REPO_ISSUES_INDEX 6. Pricing | Production-ready AI Models - AssemblyAI, https://www.assemblyai.com/pricing 7. WhisperLiveKit/docs/default_and_custom_models.md at main - GitHub, https://github.com/QuentinFuxa/WhisperLiveKit/blob/main/docs/default_and_custom_models.md 8. Human-Like AI Voice Agent: Latency, Turn-Taking, Indian Accents, https://vaniagent.com/resources/blog/human-like-ai-voice-agent 9. VEXYL-STT - Free, Self-Hosted Indian Language Speech-to-Text, https://medium.com/@anilmathewm/vexyl-stt-free-self-hosted-indian-language-speech-to-text-server-f2909003aaf6 10. Best AI Models for Hinglish Speech-to-Text in 2026 - Shunya Labs, https://www.shunyalabs.ai/blog/best-ai-models-for-hinglish-speech-to-text-in-2026 11. Indic Speech Recognition Accuracy: Published WER | AiSewak, https://aisewak.com/indic-speech-recognition-accuracy-benchmark 12. Open-Source Voice AI India 2026 â€” Sarvam, AI4Bharat, Bhasini, https://caller.digital/blog/open-source-voice-ai-india-sarvam-ai4bharat-bhasini-2026 13. Feature: Add FunASR as a self-hosted ASR backend #10073 - GitHub, https://github.com/mudler/LocalAI/issues/10073 14. Add FunASR/SenseVoice as alternative transcription engine Â· Issue, https://github.com/chidiwilliams/buzz/issues/1495 15. speech-mcp/docs/providers/funasr.md at main - GitHub, https://github.com/sandraschi/speech-mcp/blob/main/docs/providers/funasr.md 16. Feature: Add SenseVoice as ASR option â€” 5x faster, lower VRAM, https://github.com/meizhong986/WhisperJAV/issues/350 17. Feature: Add FunASR/SenseVoice as alternative STT engine #311, https://github.com/KoljaB/RealtimeSTT/issues/311 18. AssemblyAI Review 2026: Features, Pricing &amp; Alternative - Gladia, https://www.gladia.io/blog/assemblyai-review 19. Speech-to-Text APIs (2026) - APIScout, https://apiscout.dev/guides/speech-to-text-api-comparison-2026 20. Listen First, Then Answer: Timestamp-Grounded Speech Reasoning, https://arxiv.org/html/2603.19468v1 21. kullup/whisper-timestamped - Hugging Face, https://huggingface.co/kullup/whisper-timestamped 22. pyannote.audio â€” Open-Source Speaker Diarization Pipeline (2026, https://vexascribe.com/pyannote-audio 23. Whisper Timestamped: Speech-to-Text / Speech Recognition, https://builderai.tools/tool/whisper-timestamped 24. stable-ts Â· PyPI, https://pypi.org/project/stable-ts/ 25. whisper-timestamped vs whisper-wordtimestamps â€” comparison, https://www.aimodels.fyi/models/compare/whisper-timestamped-villesau-vs-whisper-wordtimestamps-collectiveai-team 26. Understanding forced ali